Lab Material
Lab 6: Configuring VM Resources
Objectives
By the end of this lab, you will be able to:

Configure VM resource allocation for CPU, memory, and disk using YAML
Modify VM resources such as CPU, memory, and disks in the VM Custom Resource (CR)
Apply changes and monitor the resource allocation of virtual machines
Understand the relationship between VM resource specifications and actual allocation
Troubleshoot common resource configuration issues
Prerequisites
Before starting this lab, you should have:

Basic understanding of Kubernetes concepts (pods, resources, YAML)
Familiarity with OpenShift CLI (oc command)
Knowledge of Linux command line operations
Completed previous labs in the OpenShift Virtualization series
Understanding of virtual machine concepts (CPU, memory, storage)
Lab Environment
Al Nafi provides Linux-based cloud machines - simply click Start Lab to begin. No need to build your own VM or install additional software. Your lab environment includes:

OpenShift cluster with Virtualization operator installed
Pre-configured storage classes
Command-line tools (oc, kubectl, vim)
Sample VM templates ready for use
Task 1: Understanding VM Resource Configuration
Subtask 1.1: Examine Current VM Resources
First, let's explore the existing VMs and their current resource allocations.

Connect to your lab environment and open a terminal

List all virtual machines in your namespace:

oc get vms
Examine a specific VM's resource configuration:
oc get vm sample-vm -o yaml
Look for the resource specifications in the output. You should see sections like:
spec:
  template:
    spec:
      domain:
        cpu:
          cores: 1
        memory:
          guest: 1Gi
        devices:
          disks:
          - disk:
              bus: virtio
            name: rootdisk
Subtask 1.2: Create a Base VM for Resource Modification
If you don't have an existing VM, create one using this configuration:

Create a new VM YAML file:
cat > base-vm.yaml << 'EOF'
apiVersion: kubevirt.io/v1
kind: VirtualMachine
metadata:
  name: resource-test-vm
  labels:
    app: resource-test
spec:
  running: false
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        kubevirt.io/vm: resource-test-vm
    spec:
      domain:
        cpu:
          cores: 1
        memory:
          guest: 1Gi
        devices:
          disks:
          - disk:
              bus: virtio
            name: rootdisk
          - disk:
              bus: virtio
            name: cloudinitdisk
          interfaces:
          - name: default
            masquerade: {}
        resources:
          requests:
            memory: 1Gi
      networks:
      - name: default
        pod: {}
      volumes:
      - name: rootdisk
        containerDisk:
          image: quay.io/kubevirt/cirros-container-disk-demo
      - name: cloudinitdisk
        cloudInitNoCloud:
          userDataBase64: SGkuXG4=
EOF
Apply the VM configuration:
oc apply -f base-vm.yaml
Verify the VM was created:
oc get vm resource-test-vm
Task 2: Modifying CPU Resources
Subtask 2.1: Configure CPU Cores
Let's modify the CPU allocation for our virtual machine.

Create a CPU-modified configuration:
cat > vm-cpu-config.yaml << 'EOF'
apiVersion: kubevirt.io/v1
kind: VirtualMachine
metadata:
  name: resource-test-vm
  labels:
    app: resource-test
spec:
  running: false
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        kubevirt.io/vm: resource-test-vm
    spec:
      domain:
        cpu:
          cores: 2
          threads: 2
          sockets: 1
        memory:
          guest: 1Gi
        devices:
          disks:
          - disk:
              bus: virtio
            name: rootdisk
          - disk:
              bus: virtio
            name: cloudinitdisk
          interfaces:
          - name: default
            masquerade: {}
        resources:
          requests:
            memory: 1Gi
            cpu: 2
          limits:
            memory: 1Gi
            cpu: 2
      networks:
      - name: default
        pod: {}
      volumes:
      - name: rootdisk
        containerDisk:
          image: quay.io/kubevirt/cirros-container-disk-demo
      - name: cloudinitdisk
        cloudInitNoCloud:
          userDataBase64: SGkuXG4=
EOF
Apply the updated configuration:
oc apply -f vm-cpu-config.yaml
Verify the CPU configuration change:
oc get vm resource-test-vm -o jsonpath='{.spec.template.spec.domain.cpu}'
Subtask 2.2: Understanding CPU Configuration Options
The CPU configuration includes several important parameters:

cores: Number of CPU cores per socket
threads: Number of threads per core
sockets: Number of CPU sockets
requests: Minimum CPU resources guaranteed
limits: Maximum CPU resources allowed
