Task 4: Configuring Disk Resources
Subtask 4.1: Add Additional Storage
Let's add more storage to our virtual machine.

Create a configuration with additional disk:
cat > vm-storage-config.yaml << 'EOF'
apiVersion: kubevirt.io/v1
kind: VirtualMachine
metadata:
  name: resource-test-vm
  labels:
    app: resource-test
spec:
  running: false
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        kubevirt.io/vm: resource-test-vm
    spec:
      domain:
        cpu:
          cores: 2
          threads: 2
          sockets: 1
        memory:
          guest: 4Gi
        devices:
          disks:
          - disk:
              bus: virtio
            name: rootdisk
          - disk:
              bus: virtio
            name: datadisk
          - disk:
              bus: virtio
            name: cloudinitdisk
          interfaces:
          - name: default
            masquerade: {}
        resources:
          requests:
            memory: 2Gi
            cpu: 1
          limits:
            memory: 4Gi
            cpu: 2
      networks:
      - name: default
        pod: {}
      volumes:
      - name: rootdisk
        containerDisk:
          image: quay.io/kubevirt/cirros-container-disk-demo
      - name: datadisk
        emptyDisk:
          capacity: 10Gi
      - name: cloudinitdisk
        cloudInitNoCloud:
          userDataBase64: SGkuXG4=
EOF
Apply the storage configuration:
oc apply -f vm-storage-config.yaml
Verify the disk configuration:
oc get vm resource-test-vm -o jsonpath='{.spec.template.spec.volumes[*].name}'
Subtask 4.2: Configure Persistent Storage
For production workloads, use persistent storage:

Create a PVC for persistent storage:
cat > vm-pvc.yaml << 'EOF'
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolumeClaim
metadata:
  name: vm-data-pvc
spec:
  accessModes:
  - ReadWriteOnce
  resources:
    requests:
      storage: 20Gi
  storageClassName: standard
EOF
Apply the PVC:
oc apply -f vm-pvc.yaml
Update VM to use persistent storage:
cat > vm-persistent-config.yaml << 'EOF'
apiVersion: kubevirt.io/v1
kind: VirtualMachine
metadata:
  name: resource-test-vm
  labels:
    app: resource-test
spec:
  running: false
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        kubevirt.io/vm: resource-test-vm
    spec:
      domain:
        cpu:
          cores: 2
          threads: 2
          sockets: 1
        memory:
          guest: 4Gi
        devices:
          disks:
          - disk:
              bus: virtio
            name: rootdisk
          - disk:
              bus: virtio
            name: persistentdisk
          - disk:
              bus: virtio
            name: cloudinitdisk
          interfaces:
          - name: default
            masquerade: {}
        resources:
          requests:
            memory: 2Gi
            cpu: 1
          limits:
            memory: 4Gi
            cpu: 2
      networks:
      - name: default
        pod: {}
      volumes:
      - name: rootdisk
        containerDisk:
          image: quay.io/kubevirt/cirros-container-disk-demo
      - name: persistentdisk
        persistentVolumeClaim:
          claimName: vm-data-pvc
      - name: cloudinitdisk
        cloudInitNoCloud:
          userDataBase64: SGkuXG4=
EOF
Apply the persistent storage configuration:
oc apply -f vm-persistent-config.yaml
Task 5: Monitoring Resource Allocation
Subtask 5.1: Start the VM and Monitor Resources
Start the virtual machine:
oc patch vm resource-test-vm --type merge -p '{"spec":{"running":true}}'
Wait for the VM to start:
oc get vm resource-test-vm -w
Press Ctrl+C when the VM shows Running: True.

Check the VMI (Virtual Machine Instance):
oc get vmi resource-test-vm
Subtask 5.2: Monitor Resource Usage
View detailed resource information:
oc describe vmi resource-test-vm
Check the underlying pod resources:
oc get pods -l kubevirt.io/vm=resource-test-vm -o wide
Monitor resource usage in real-time:
oc top pods -l kubevirt.io/vm=resource-test-vm
Subtask 5.3: Verify Resource Allocation
Check CPU allocation:
oc get vmi resource-test-vm -o jsonpath='{.spec.domain.cpu}' | jq
Check memory allocation:
oc get vmi resource-test-vm -o jsonpath='{.spec.domain.memory}' | jq
List all disks:
oc get vmi resource-test-vm -o jsonpath='{.spec.volumes[*].name}'
Task 6: Dynamic Resource Modification
Subtask 6.1: Hot-plug Memory (if supported)
Some resource changes can be made while the VM is running:

Check if hot-plug is supported:
oc get vmi resource-test-vm -o jsonpath='{.status.conditions[?(@.type=="LiveMigratable")].status}'
Attempt to increase memory (this may require stopping the VM):
oc patch vm resource-test-vm --type merge -p '{"spec":{"template":{"spec":{"domain":{"memory":{"guest":"6Gi"}}}}}}'
Subtask 6.2: Restart VM with New Configuration
Stop the VM:
oc patch vm resource-test-vm --type merge -p '{"spec":{"running":false}}'
Wait for VM to stop:
oc get vm resource-test-vm -w
Start the VM with new configuration:
oc patch vm resource-test-vm --type merge -p '{"spec":{"running":true}}'
Verify the new resource allocation:
oc get vmi resource-test-vm -o jsonpath='{.spec.domain.memory.guest}'
Troubleshooting Common Issues
Issue 1: Insufficient Resources
If your VM fails to start due to insufficient resources:

Check node resources:
oc describe nodes
Reduce resource requests:
oc patch vm resource-test-vm --type merge -p '{"spec":{"template":{"spec":{"domain":{"resources":{"requests":{"memory":"512Mi","cpu":"500m"}}}}}}}'
Issue 2: Storage Issues
If storage-related problems occur:

Check PVC status:
oc get pvc vm-data-pvc
Verify storage class:
oc get storageclass
Check available storage:
oc get pv
Issue 3: Configuration Validation Errors
If YAML validation fails:

Validate YAML syntax:
oc apply -f vm-config.yaml --dry-run=client
Check resource quotas:
oc describe quota
Cleanup
After completing the lab, clean up the resources:

Stop and delete the VM:
oc delete vm resource-test-vm
Delete the PVC:
oc delete pvc vm-data-pvc
Remove configuration files:
rm -f *.yaml
Conclusion
In this lab, you have successfully learned how to configure VM resources in OpenShift Virtualization. You accomplished the following key tasks:

Configured CPU resources including cores, threads, and sockets
Modified memory allocation with both basic and overcommit scenarios
Added storage resources using both ephemeral and persistent volumes
Applied resource changes using YAML configurations
Monitored resource allocation and verified configurations
Troubleshot common issues related to resource configuration
These skills are essential for managing virtual machines in production environments, allowing you to optimize performance, ensure resource availability, and maintain efficient resource utilization. Understanding how to properly configure VM resources helps ensure your virtual workloads run smoothly while making the best use of your cluster's available resources.

The ability to modify VM resources dynamically and monitor their allocation is crucial for maintaining high-performance virtualized environments in OpenShift, making you better prepared for real-world scenarios and certification requirements.
